<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S1">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2><button c=
lass=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding=
 element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p1.1">Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL)=
 has seen significant success in recent years, with applications in real-ti=
me strategy games&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Arulku=
maran et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405=
.11778v1#bib.bib4" title=3D"">2019</a>; Ye et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref=
" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib58" title=3D"">2020</a=
>)</cite>, card games&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ba=
rd et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11=
778v1#bib.bib5" title=3D"">2020</a>)</cite>, sports games&nbsp;<cite class=
=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Kurach et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref"=
 href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib26" title=3D"">2020</a>=
)</cite>, autonomous driving&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cit=
ep">(Zhou et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/=
2405.11778v1#bib.bib63" title=3D"">2020</a>)</cite>, and multi-robot naviga=
tion&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Long et&nbsp;al., <=
a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib30" =
title=3D"">2018</a>)</cite>. Nonetheless, challenges within multi-agent env=
ironments have led to the problem of sample inefficiency. One key issue is =
the non-stationarity of multi-agent settings, where agents continuously upd=
ate their policies based on observations and rewards, resulting in a changi=
ng environment for individual agents&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citem=
acro_citep">(Nguyen et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv=
.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib36" title=3D"">2020</a>)</cite>. Additionally=
, the joint action space=E2=80=99s dimension can exponentially increase wit=
h the number of agents, leading to an immense policy search space&nbsp;<cit=
e class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Hernandez-Leal et&nbsp;al., <a cl=
ass=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib18" titl=
e=3D"">2020</a>)</cite>. These challenges, combined with issues such as par=
tial observation, coordination, and credit assignment, necessitate a consid=
erable demand for samples in MARL for effective training&nbsp;<cite class=
=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Gronauer &amp; Diepold, <a class=3D"ltx_=
ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib11" title=3D"">2022=
</a>)</cite>.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">=
Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S1.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p2.1">Conversely, Model-Based Reinforcement Lea=
rning (MBRL) has demonstrated its worth in terms of sample efficiency withi=
n single-agent RL scenarios, both in practice&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite =
ltx_citemacro_citep">(Wang et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:=
//arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib54" title=3D"">2019</a>)</cite> and th=
eory&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Sun et&nbsp;al., <a=
 class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib47" t=
itle=3D"">2019</a>)</cite>. Unlike model-free methods, MBRL approaches typi=
cally focus on learning a parameterized model that characterizes the transi=
tion or reward functions of the real environment&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_ci=
te ltx_citemacro_citep">(Sutton &amp; Barto, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"h=
ttps://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib49" title=3D"">2018</a>; Corneil =
et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778=
v1#bib.bib8" title=3D"">2018</a>; Ha &amp; Schmidhuber, <a class=3D"ltx_ref=
" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib12" title=3D"">2018</a=
>)</cite>.
Based on the usage of the learned model, MBRL methods can be roughly divide=
d into two lines: planning with model&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cite=
macro_citep">(Hewing et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxi=
v.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib20" title=3D"">2020</a>; Nagabandi et&nbsp;a=
l., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bi=
b34" title=3D"">2018</a>; Wang &amp; Ba, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https=
://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib53" title=3D"">2019</a>; Schrittwiese=
r et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.117=
78v1#bib.bib42" title=3D"">2020</a>; Hansen et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_re=
f" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib16" title=3D"">2022</=
a>)</cite> and data augmentation with model&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite lt=
x_citemacro_citep">(Kurutach et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"http=
s://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib27" title=3D"">2018</a>; Janner et&n=
bsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#b=
ib.bib22" title=3D"">2019</a>; Hafner et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" hre=
f=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib13" title=3D"">2019</a>; <a=
 class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib14" t=
itle=3D"">2020</a>; <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/240=
5.11778v1#bib.bib15" title=3D"">2023</a>)</cite>. Due to the foresight inhe=
rent in planning methods and their theoretically guaranteed convergence pro=
perties, MBRL with planning often demonstrates significantly higher sample =
efficiency and converges more rapidly&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cite=
macro_citep">(Zhang et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv=
.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib62" title=3D"">2020</a>)</cite>. A well-known=
 planning-based MBRL method is MuZero, which conducts Monte Carlo Tree Sear=
ch (MCTS) with a value-equivalent learned model and achieves superhuman per=
formance in video games like Atari and board games including Go, Chess and =
Shogi&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Schrittwieser et&n=
bsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#b=
ib.bib42" title=3D"">2020</a>)</cite>.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" =
style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S1.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p3.1">Despite the success of MBRL in single-age=
nt settings, planning with the multi-agent environment model remains in its=
 early stages of development. Recent efforts have emerged to bridge the gap=
 by combining single-agent MBRL algorithms with MARL frameworks&nbsp;<cite =
class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Willemsen et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"=
ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib56" title=3D"">=
2021</a>; Bargiacchi et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxi=
v.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib6" title=3D"">2021</a>; Mahajan et&nbsp;al.,=
 <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib32=
" title=3D"">2021</a>; Egorov &amp; Shpilman, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"=
https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib10" title=3D"">2022</a>)</cite>.=
 However, the extension of single-agent MBRL methods to multi-agent setting=
s presents formidable challenges. On one hand, existing model designs for s=
ingle-agent algorithms do not account for the unique biases inherent to mul=
ti-agent environments, such as the nearly-independent property of agents. C=
onsequently, directly employing models from single-agent MBRL algorithms ty=
pically falls short in supporting efficient learning within real-world mult=
i-agent environments, underscoring the paramount importance of model redesi=
gn. On the other hand, multi-agent environments possess action spaces signi=
ficantly more intricate than their single-agent counterparts, thereby expon=
entially escalating the search complexity within multi-agent settings. This=
 necessitates exploration into specialized planning algorithms tailored to =
these complex action spaces.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"d=
isplay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S1.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p4.1">In this paper, we propose MAZero, the fir=
st empirically effective approach that extends the MuZero paradigm into mul=
ti-agent cooperative environments. In particular, our contributions are fou=
rfold:</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report =
issue for preceding element</button>
<ol class=3D"ltx_enumerate" id=3D"S1.I1">
<li class=3D"ltx_item" id=3D"S1.I1.i1" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.I1.i1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.I1.i1.p1.1">Inspired by the Centralized Trainin=
g with Decentralized Execution (CTDE) concept, we develop a centralized-val=
ue, individual-dynamic model with shared parameters among all agents. We in=
corporate an additional communication block using the attention mechanism t=
o promote cooperation during the model unrolling (see Section&nbsp;<a class=
=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#S4.SS1" title=3D"4=
.1 Model Structure =E2=80=A3 4 MAZero algorithm =E2=80=A3 Efficient Multi-a=
gent Reinforcement Learning by Planning"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_ta=
g">4.1</span></a>).</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: n=
one;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</li>
<li class=3D"ltx_item" id=3D"S1.I1.i2" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.I1.i2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.I1.i2.p1.2">Given the deterministic characteris=
tics of the learned model, we have devised the Optimistic Search Lambda (OS=
(<math alttext=3D"\lambda" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S1.I=
1.i2.p1.1.m1.1"><semantics id=3D"S1.I1.i2.p1.1.m1.1a"><mi id=3D"S1.I1.i2.p1=
.1.m1.1.1" xref=3D"S1.I1.i2.p1.1.m1.1.1.cmml">=CE=BB</mi><annotation-xml en=
