<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S4.SS2">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Optimistic Search Lam=
bda</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report is=
sue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S4.SS2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1">Having discerned that MAZero has acqu=
ired a deterministic world model, we devise a Optimistic Search Lambda (OS(=
<math alttext=3D"\lambda" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.SS=
2.p1.1.m1.1"><semantics id=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1.1a"><mi id=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1.1=
.1" xref=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1.1.1.cmml">=CE=BB</mi><annotation-xml encoding=3D=
"MathML-Content" id=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1.1b"><ci id=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1.1.1.cmml=
" xref=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1.1.1">=F0=9D=9C=86</ci></annotation-xml><annotation=
 encoding=3D"application/x-tex" id=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1.1c">\lambda</annotatio=
n><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-llamapun" id=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1.m1.1d">=
italic_=CE=BB</annotation></semantics></math>)) approach to better harness =
this characteristic of the model.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=
=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S4.SS2.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.SS2.p2.1">In previous endeavors, the selection =
stage of MCTS employed two metrics (value score and exploration bonus) to g=
auge our interest in a particular action.
In this context, the value score utilized the mean estimate of values obtai=
ned from all simulations within that node=E2=80=99s subtree.
However, within the deterministic model, the mean estimate appears excessiv=
ely conservative.
Contemplating a scenario where the tree degenerates into a multi-armed band=
it, if pulling an arm consistently yields a deterministic outcome, there ar=
ises no necessity, akin to the Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) algorithm, to r=
epeatedly sample the same arm and calculate its average.
Similarly, within the tree-based version of the UCT (Upper Confidence Trees=
) algorithm, averaging values across all simulations in a subtree may be su=
bstituted with a more optimistic estimation when the environment is determi=
nistic.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report=
 issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S4.SS2.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.SS2.p3.1">Hence, courtesy of the deterministic =
model, our focus narrows to managing model generalization errors rather tha=
n contending with errors introduced by environmental stochasticity.
Built upon this conceptual foundation, we have devised a methodology for ca=
lculating the value score that places a heightened emphasis on optimistic v=
alues.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report =
issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S4.SS2.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1">Specifically, for each node <math alt=
text=3D"s" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1.1"><s=
emantics id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1.1a"><mi id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1.1.1" xref=3D"S4.=
SS2.p4.1.m1.1.1.cmml">s</mi><annotation-xml encoding=3D"MathML-Content" id=
=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1.1b"><ci id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1.1.1.cmml" xref=3D"S4.SS2.p4=
.1.m1.1.1">=F0=9D=91=A0</ci></annotation-xml><annotation encoding=3D"applic=
ation/x-tex" id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1.1c">s</annotation><annotation encoding=3D=
"application/x-llamapun" id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1.1d">italic_s</annotation></se=
mantics></math>, we define:</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"di=
splay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
