<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S5">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2><button cl=
ass=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding =
element</button>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S5.SS1">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>StarCraft Multi-Agent=
 Challenge</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Re=
port issue for preceding element</button>
<section class=3D"ltx_paragraph" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1">
<h5 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines</h5><button class=3D"=
sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element=
</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1">We compare MAZero with both =
model-based and model-free baseline methods on StarCraft Multi-Agent Challe=
nge&nbsp;(SMAC) environments. The model-based baseline is MAMBA&nbsp;<cite =
class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Egorov &amp; Shpilman, <a class=3D"=
ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib10" title=3D"">=
2022</a>)</cite>, a recently introduced multi-agent MARL algorithm based on=
 DreamerV2&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Hafner et&nbs=
p;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib=
.bib14" title=3D"">2020</a>)</cite> known for its state-of-the-art sample e=
fficiency in various SMAC tasks. The model-free MARL methods includes QMIX&=
nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Rashid et&nbsp;al., <a c=
lass=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib39" tit=
le=3D"">2020</a>)</cite>, QPLEX&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_=
citep">(Wang et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/ht=
ml/2405.11778v1#bib.bib52" title=3D"">2020a</a>)</cite>, RODE&nbsp;<cite cl=
ass=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Wang et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref=
" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib55" title=3D"">2020b</=
a>)</cite>, CDS&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Li et&nb=
sp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bi=
b.bib28" title=3D"">2021</a>)</cite>, and MAPPO&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cit=
e ltx_citemacro_citep">(Yu et&nbsp;al., <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:=
//arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#bib.bib61" title=3D"">2022</a>)</cite>.</p><b=
utton class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for pr=
eceding element</button>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"S5.F3"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"344" id=3D"S5=
.F3.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1/x3.png" width=3D"830">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Comparisons against baselines in SMAC. Y axis =
denotes the win rate and X axis denotes the number of steps taken in the en=
vironment. Each algorithm is executed with 10 random seeds.</figcaption>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1"><a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D=
"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#S5.F3" title=3D"In Baselines =E2=80=A3=
 5.1 StarCraft Multi-Agent Challenge =E2=80=A3 5 Experiments =E2=80=A3 Effi=
cient Multi-agent Reinforcement Learning by Planning"><span class=3D"ltx_te=
xt ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span>&nbsp;<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</=
span></a> illustrates the results in the SMAC environments. Among the 23 av=
ailable scenarios, we have chosen 8 for presentation in this paper. Specifi=
cally, we have selected four random scenarios categorized as <span class=3D=
"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1">Easy</span> tasks =
and 4 <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2=
">Hard</span> tasks. It is evident that MAZero outperforms all baseline alg=
orithms across eight scenarios with a given number of steps taken in the en=
vironment. Notably, both MAZero and MAMBA, which are categorized as model-b=
ased methods, exhibit markedly superior sample efficiency in easy tasks whe=
n compared to model-free baselines. Furthermore, MAZero displays a more sta=
ble training curve and smoother win rate during evaluation than MAMBA. In t=
he realm of hard tasks, MAZero surpasses MAMBA in terms of overall performa=
nce, with a noteworthy emphasis on the <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_ita=
lic" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3">2c_vs_64zg</span> scenario. In this part=
icular scenario, MAZero attains a higher win rate with a significantly redu=
ced sample size when contrasted with other methods. This scenario involves =
a unique property, featuring only two agents and an expansive action space =
of up to 70 for each agent, in contrast to other scenarios where the agent=
=E2=80=99s action space is generally fewer than 20. This distinctive charac=
teristic enhances the role of planning in MAZero components, similar to MuZ=
ero=E2=80=99s outstanding performance in the domain of Go.</p><button class=
=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding ele=
ment</button>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"S5.F4"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"307" id=3D"S5=
.F4.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1/x4.png" width=3D"830">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Comparisons against MBRL baselines in SMAC. Th=
e y-axis denotes the win rate, and the X-axis denotes the cumulative run ti=
me of algorithms in the same platform.</figcaption>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1">As MAZero builds upon the Mu=
Zero framework, we perform end-to-end training directly using planning resu=
lts from the replay buffer. Consequently, this approach circumvents the tim=
e overhead for data augmentation, as seen in Dreamer-based methods. <a clas=
s=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1#S5.F4" title=3D"I=
n Baselines =E2=80=A3 5.1 StarCraft Multi-Agent Challenge =E2=80=A3 5 Exper=
iments =E2=80=A3 Efficient Multi-agent Reinforcement Learning by Planning">=
<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span>&nbsp;<span class=3D"ltx_=
text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the superior performance of MAZe=
ro with respect to the temporal cost in SMAC environments when compared to =
MAMBA.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report =
issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S5.SS2">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Ablation</h3><button =
class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for precedin=
g element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2">We perform several ablation experimen=
ts on the two proposed techniques: OS(<math alttext=3D"\lambda" class=3D"lt=
x_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.1.m1.1"><semantics id=3D"S5.SS2.=
p1.1.m1.1a"><mi id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.1.m1.1.1" xref=3D"S5.SS2.p1.1.m1.1.1.cmml">=
=CE=BB</mi><annotation-xml encoding=3D"MathML-Content" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.1.m1=
.1b"><ci id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.1.m1.1.1.cmml" xref=3D"S5.SS2.p1.1.m1.1.1">=F0=9D=
=9C=86</ci></annotation-xml><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex" id=
=3D"S5.SS2.p1.1.m1.1c">\lambda</annotation><annotation encoding=3D"applicat=
ion/x-llamapun" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.1.m1.1d">italic_=CE=BB</annotation></semant=
ics></math>) and AWPO. The results with algorithms executed with three rand=
om seeds are reported in <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/htm=
l/2405.11778v1#S5.F5" title=3D"In 5.2 Ablation =E2=80=A3 5 Experiments =E2=
=80=A3 Efficient Multi-agent Reinforcement Learning by Planning"><span clas=
s=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span>&nbsp;<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_r=
ef_tag">5</span></a>. In particular, we examine whether disabling OS(<math =
alttext=3D"\lambda" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2=
.m2.1"><semantics id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2.m2.1a"><mi id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2.m2.1.1" xr=
ef=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2.m2.1.1.cmml">=CE=BB</mi><annotation-xml encoding=3D"MathM=
L-Content" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2.m2.1b"><ci id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2.m2.1.1.cmml" xref=
=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2.m2.1.1">=F0=9D=9C=86</ci></annotation-xml><annotation encod=
ing=3D"application/x-tex" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2.m2.1c">\lambda</annotation><ann=
otation encoding=3D"application/x-llamapun" id=3D"S5.SS2.p1.2.m2.1d">italic=
_=CE=BB</annotation></semantics></math>), AWPO, or both of them (i.e., usin=
g original Sampled MCTS and BC loss function) impairs final performance. Si=
gnificantly, both techniques greatly impact final performance and learning =
efficiency.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Re=
port issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"S5.F5"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"162" id=3D"S5=
.F5.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1/x5.png" width=3D"581">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Ablation study on proposed approaches for plan=
ning.</figcaption>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S5.SS2.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S5.SS2.p2.1">In our ablation of the MAZero network=
 structure (<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v=
1#S5.F6" title=3D"In 5.2 Ablation =E2=80=A3 5 Experiments =E2=80=A3 Efficie=
nt Multi-agent Reinforcement Learning by Planning"><span class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span>&nbsp;<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</spa=
n></a>), we have discerned the substantial impact of two components, =E2=80=
=9Ccommunication=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Csharing=E2=80=9D, on algorithmic per=
formance. In stark contrast, the flattened model employed in single-agent M=
BRL, due to its failure to encapsulate the unique biases of multi-agent env=
ironments, can only learn victorious strategies in the most rudimentary of =
scenarios, such as the <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S5.SS=
2.p2.1.1">2m_vs_1z</span> map.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D=
"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"S5.F6"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"231" id=3D"S5=
.F6.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11778v1/x6.png" width=3D"830">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Ablation study on network structure.</figcapti=
on>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
</section>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S6">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2><button cla=
ss=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding e=
lement</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"S6.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S6.p1.1">In this paper, we introduce a model-based=
 multi-agent algorithm, MAZero, which utilizes the CTDE framework and MCTS =
planning. This approach boasts superior sample efficiency compared to state=
-of-the-art model-free methods and provides comparable or better performanc=
e than existing model-based methods in terms of both sample and computation=
al efficiency. We also develop two novel approaches, OS(<math alttext=3D"\l=
ambda" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S6.p1.1.m1.1"><semantics=
 id=3D"S6.p1.1.m1.1a"><mi id=3D"S6.p1.1.m1.1.1" xref=3D"S6.p1.1.m1.1.1.cmml=
">=CE=BB</mi><annotation-xml encoding=3D"MathML-Content" id=3D"S6.p1.1.m1.1=
b"><ci id=3D"S6.p1.1.m1.1.1.cmml" xref=3D"S6.p1.1.m1.1.1">=F0=9D=9C=86</ci>=
</annotation-xml><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex" id=3D"S6.p1.1.m=
1.1c">\lambda</annotation><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-llamapun" i=
